---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { providerById, terms } from "../lib/data";
---

<Base
  title="Same word, different meaning"
  description="Pause, stop, sleep, snapshot, fork, persistent, archive and credits mean different things at different providers."
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <p class="kicker">Vocabulary</p>
    <h1>Same word, different meaning</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Eight words do most of the damage in this market. Each one means something specific at each
      provider, and reading a competitor's meaning into it changes what you think you are buying.
    </p>
  </header>

  <nav class="measure" aria-label="Terms">
    <ul>
      {terms.map((term) => <li><a href={`#${term.term.toLowerCase()}`}>{term.term}</a></li>)}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  {terms.map((term) => (
    <section id={term.term.toLowerCase()}>
      <h2>{term.term}</h2>
      <p class="standfirst">{term.short}</p>
      <p class="measure">{term.whyItMatters}</p>

      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Provider</th>
              <th scope="col">What the word means there</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {term.meanings.map((meaning) => (
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">
                  <a href={`/providers/${meaning.provider}/`}>
                    {providerById.get(meaning.provider)?.name ?? meaning.provider}
                  </a>
                </th>
                <td>{meaning.meaning}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="measure"><strong>What to check:</strong> {term.checkFor}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <div class="callout">
    <p class="callout-title">The pattern</p>
    <p>
      Almost every confusion here comes from one question: what survives, and for how long? A
      “paused” machine may keep memory, keep only files, or not exist. A “snapshot” may include
      memory or not. “Persistent” may mean the whole disk, one folder, or fourteen days.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
